Excerpt from the organization's governance charter, Section 7 (Deliberation Records).

7.1 Openness of process. The charter holds that institutional legitimacy depends on the ability of any member to see how a decision was reached. Where a deliberation concerns shared operations or shared risk, the organization prefers that the record of the discussion be available to members once the matter is closed, so that a decision can be questioned on its merits rather than on the identity of who argued it.

7.2 Reviewability after the fact. Decisions that carry organizational risk must remain reviewable once the deliberation ends. The charter requires that the reasoning behind such a decision be attributable to a process that others can inspect, and that the final recommendation be accompanied by the grounds on which it rests.

7.3 Retention and access. The charter also recognizes that not every record is safe to hold or to show. Records that name individuals in a sensitive context, that describe unreleased systems, or that touch security-relevant architecture must be retained under access control, with a defined retention period and a path for revocation, rather than left in an open, permanent, and unredacted form.

7.4 Confidentiality carve-out. Nothing in Section 7 requires that the raw working record of a deliberation be published. The charter distinguishes the published recommendation (the decision and its grounds) from the underlying raw record (individual arguments, citations, and in-progress positions), and assigns them different handling.

7.5 Appeal. A member who believes a ruling or recommendation was reached in error may raise that concern to the orchestrator within one round of the record closing; the appeal is heard on the recorded arguments.
